course: dictionary
lesson: 1785
title: Clinically: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The same word can have different meanings. «Clinically» is one of these words. Its main meaning is in a medical way, the way doctors check patients. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, because they are its context.
explain: «The cream was clinically tested on people before it went on sale.» The clue is tested on people, which is a careful medical check, so clinically means in a medical way. Always ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: «The nurse told him the news clinically, with no smile and no feeling in her voice.» The clue is no feeling, so clinically means cold and calm, like a hospital. In football, «He finished clinically» means he scored very accurately. Same word, three meanings: the context decides which one you need.
fact: «Clinically» means in a medical way, as judged by doctors examining patients.
fact: «Clinically proven» and «clinically tested» mean a product was tried on people in a careful study.
fact: «Clinically» can also mean cold and calm, with no strong feelings shown, like a hospital.
fact: In sport, «finished clinically» means scored very efficiently and accurately.
fact: Adverts use «clinically proven» to sound trustworthy, so ask who tested it, on how many people, and compared with what.
exercise: «The cream was clinically tested on people before it went on sale.» What does clinically tested mean here? | *Tested in a careful medical way, on people | Tested for fun at a party | Tested by a robot in a game | Tested quickly by a friend in the kitchen
exercise: «The doctor gave the news clinically, without any feeling in her voice.» What does clinically suggest? | Warm and loving | Happy and excited | Very loudly | *Cold and calm, with no feelings shown
exercise: A football report says «He finished clinically.» What does this mean? | He ran away from the ball | *He scored very accurately and efficiently | He fell over and cried | He kicked the ball into the crowd
test: An advert says «clinically proven». What should you ask? | Whether the packet is a nice colour | Whether the advert has music | *Who tested it, on how many people, and compared with what? | How much the shop is charging
test: «Clinically obese» is a phrase you may see in health lessons. What kind of phrase is it? | A sports word about goals | A cookbook word about food | *A medical term used by doctors | A word about the weather
exam: Which meaning of clinically fits «The surgeon described the wound clinically, without flinching»? | Sleepily, in bed | *Coldly and calmly, with no emotion shown | Warmly and with a big smile | Noisily, like a crowd
